<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiroshima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiroshima is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. On 6 August 1945 it became the first city to be destroyed by an atomic bomb, dropped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The bombing, followed by that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, preceded Japan's surrender and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiroshima.jpg" alt="Hiroshima" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atomic_Bomb_Dome_and_Motoyaso_River,_Hiroshima,_Northwest_view_20190417_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was originally a small fishing village on the shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-bay">Hiroshima Bay</a>, which from the 12th century prospered through its ties to a Zen Buddhist temple, Mitaki-dera, and through growing trade with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a>. The warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> founded Hiroshima in 1589 on the delta coast of the Seto Inland Sea, and the name means wide island. His castle was quickly built, and he moved into it in 1593. After Terumoto sided with the losers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> took most of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, including Hiroshima, and gave Aki Province to the daimyō Masanori Fukushima. From 1619 until 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asano-clan">Asano clan</a> ruled the city.</p><p>After the han was abolished in 1871 the city became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-prefecture">Hiroshima Prefecture</a> and, with the building of Ujina Harbor in the 1880s, an important port. The San'yō Railway reached it in 1894. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> the government moved there temporarily, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> kept his headquarters at Hiroshima Castle from 15 September 1894 to 27 April 1895. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Second General Army and the Chūgoku Regional Army were headquartered there, and the port was a key centre for shipping and supply depots.</p><p>At 8:15 a.m. on Monday, 6 August 1945, the American B-29 Enola Gay, flown by Paul Tibbets, dropped the bomb Little Boy on the city. It directly killed at least 70,000 people, among them thousands of Korean forced labourers, and fewer than 10 per cent of the casualties were military. The population before the bombing was about 345,000. About 70 per cent of the buildings were destroyed and another 7 per cent badly damaged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hiroshima is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-prefecture">Hiroshima Prefecture</a> and the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. In 1949 the national parliament proclaimed it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">City of Peace</a> and passed the Hiroshima Peace Memorial City Construction Law to help rebuild it. The Peace Memorial Park was designed that year, and the Peace Memorial Museum opened in 1955. The Genbaku Dome, formerly the Prefectural Industrial Promotion Hall and the building closest to the explosion that survived, is part of the Hiroshima Peace Memorial, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> since 1996. The castle was rebuilt in 1958. The population, which had dropped to 137,197 after the bombing, had returned to its prewar level by 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hiroshima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The atomic bombing of <b>Hiroshima</b> on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August, combined with the Soviet declaration of war on 8 August and the rapid destruction of the Kwantung Army in Manchuria, finally gave Emperor Hirohito the external shock he needed to override institutional resistance to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the atomic bombings of <b>Hiroshima</b> and Nagasaki, where many Korean labourers were among the victims, and the Soviet invasion of Manchuria, Japan surrendered on 15 August 1945, ending 35 years of colonial rule; Japanese troops remained in the south until mid-September.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Front in 1918, and in the Second World War, which the United States entered after the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, its industry and forces fought in Europe and the Pacific, and the war ended after atomic bombs were dropped on <b>Hiroshima</b> and Nagasaki.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1999, after a <b>Hiroshima</b> school principal killed himself over a dispute between his school board and his teachers about the flag and anthem, the government of Keizō Obuchi passed the Act on National Flag and Anthem, enacted on 13 August 1999 after a 403 to 86 vote…</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>The B-29 Enola Gay dropped a uranium bomb on <b>Hiroshima</b>, the first nuclear weapon used in war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-6">6 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its yield of about 50 megatons was some 3,000 times that of the <b>Hiroshima</b> bomb.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
